#### Receivables advance rates

| Receivable type | Maximum advance rate |
|---|---|
| Eligible **domestic** A/R | **80%** |
| Eligible **insured foreign** A/R | **90%** |
| Eligible A/R on **prime Federal contracts** | **90%** |

To be a **prime Federal contract**, the Lender must have an **assignment of contract proceeds under the Assignment of Claims Act of 1940** (31 U.S.C. 3727), **or** the Borrower is a subcontractor, the prime has an assignment under the Act, and proceeds are disbursed by a **third-party funds control facility**. Insured foreign A/R qualifies for 90% when insured by the **Export-Import Bank or a major private insurer**.

Other exceptions need **SBA's prior written concurrence**. The advance rate **should not include any net profit**. Factors in setting the rate: the Borrower's control and accounting systems; enhancements such as credit insurance; age of receivables; credit quality and credit policy; turnover history; industry orientation and condition; and net profit margin.

#### Receivables that never count

- Due **more than 180 calendar days from invoice** under original terms, unless supported by acceptable **letters of credit or credit insurance**;
- Subject to any **adjustment, deduction, defense, dispute or counterclaim**, or contingent in any way;
- The portion due from a **credit-insured buyer above the policy limit**;
- Due from an **affiliated company**;
- Due from a foreign buyer in a country SBA is **legally prohibited** from doing business with (Note #7 of the **EXIM Country Limitation Schedule**);
- Deemed **uncollectible or unacceptable** (returned, rejected or repossessed items);
- From **bill-and-hold, guarantee sale, sale-and-return, sale on approval, consignment** or any repurchase/return basis, or evidenced by **chattel paper**;
- **Contra accounts**: reduce eligible A/R by the **entire** amount of the payable.

#### Inventory advance rate

The maximum is **50% of eligible inventory**, and **no advances on inventory outside the United States**. Exceptions need SBA's prior written concurrence; consider material and labor cost (or invoice cost less discounts), nature of the product, product liability, manufacturer buyback agreements, and physical location (single sites are easier to control).

#### Inventory that never counts

- Not subject to a valid, perfected, enforceable **first priority lien** in favor of the Lender;
- At an address **not disclosed to the Lender in writing**;
- **Not located in the United States**;
- On **consignment** (placed or held);
- **Demonstration** inventory;
- **Proprietary software, spare parts** or other inventory **not intended for resale**;
- **Damaged, obsolete, returned, defective, recalled** or unfit for processing.

#### Adjusting rates after closing

After initial disbursement, Lenders have **unilateral authority** to raise or lower the advance rate for **receivables** and for **inventory** by **as much as 5%** above or below the rate in the SBA Loan System. Larger changes need **SBA's prior written concurrence**.
